me\\n**Adjectives** (family-friendly, upbeat):\\n- Cosmic, Groovy, Jazzy, Mighty, Stellar, Radical, Tubular, Gnarly, Bodacious, Wicked\\n- Fantastic, Amazing, Super, Ultra, Mega, Epic, Legendary, Awesome, Marvelous, Incredible\\n- Sparkly, Nifty, Snazzy, Zippy, Zesty, Funky, Peppy, Perky, Chipper, Spunky\\n- Rockin\', Jammin\', Kickin\', Swingin\', Cruisin\', Chillin\', Vibin\', Rollin\', Bouncin\', Groovin\'\\n\\n**Nouns** (pop culture icons, characters, objects):\\n- Astronaut, Robot, Unicorn, Wizard, Ninja, Pirate, Superhero, Viking, Cowboy, Samurai\\n- Panda, Penguin, Phoenix, Dragon, Tiger, Eagle, Falcon, Wolf, Bear, Lion\\n- Rockstar, DJ, Dancer, Magician, Acrobat, Champion, Hero, Legend, Maverick, Ace\\n- Comet, Meteor, Galaxy, Nebula, Supernova, Stardust, Moonbeam, Sunray, Rainbow, Thunder\\n\\n### Implementation Approach\\n1. **Frontend utility function** in JavaScript that:\\n   - Takes an agent ID string\\n   - Hashes it deterministically\\n   - Uses the hash to select adjective and noun from word lists\\n   - Returns the whimsical name\\n\\n2. **UI Integration**:\\n   - Display whimsical name as the primary identifier\\n   - Show technical ID in smaller/muted text or tooltip\\n   - Apply in these components:\\n     - `AgentHierarchyTree.jsx` - tree view of agents\\n     - `AgentStatusPane.jsx` - detailed agent view\\n     - `AgentSummaryPane.jsx` - summary cards\\n     - `ActivityFeed.jsx` - activity entries\\n\\n### Visual Design\\n- Whimsical name: Bold, primary text color\\n- Technical ID: Small, muted gray, shown below or in parentheses\\n- Optional: Add emoji based on agent type (e.g., \\ud83d\\udc51 for executive, \\ud83d\\udd27 for developer)\\n\\n## Technical Requirements\\n\\n### New Files\\n1. `frontend/src/utils/whimsicalNames.js` - Name generation utility\\n\\n### Modified Files\\n1. `frontend/src/components/AgentHierarchyTree.jsx` - Use whimsical names\\n2. `frontend/src/components/AgentStatusPane.jsx` - Use whimsical names\\n3. `frontend/src/components/AgentSummaryPane.jsx` - Use whimsical names\\n4. `frontend/src/components/ActivityFeed.jsx` - Use whimsical names\\n\\n## Success Criteria\\n1. \\u2705 All agent identifiers display whimsical names in the UI\\n2. \\u2705 Names are deterministic (same ID = same name every time)\\n3. \\u2705 Technical IDs remain accessible (tooltip or secondary text)\\n4. \\u2705 Names are family-friendly American pop culture references\\n5. \\u2705 All existing tests pass\\n6. \\u2705 UI remains functional with no regressions\\n\\n## Out of Scope\\n- Backend changes (names are UI-only display transformation)\\n- Persisting name mappings (calculated on-the-fly from hash)\\n- User customization of names\\n- Agent \\"avatars\\" or icons (beyond simple emojis)\\n\\n## Constraints\\n- Must not break existing functionality\\n- Must maintain ability to debug using technical IDs\\n- Names must be consistent across page refreshes\\n"}'}]}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514', 'system': 'You are a System Architect agent.\n\n## Purpose\nDesigns the show formations and execution strategy. Designs software architecture based on requirements documents. Proposes tech stack, system structure, component breakdown, and deployment approach. Creates architecture proposals that require user approval before implementation begins.\n\n## Instructions\nYou are a software architect. Your job is to design a robust, maintainable architecture based on requirements. Your proposal will be reviewed by the user before implementation begins.\n\n### Your Process:\n\n**BE DECISIVE**: Make architectural decisions based on industry best practices. Only escalate when major trade-offs exist (e.g., SQL vs NoSQL for specific use case with conflicting requirements).\n\n**Default Choices** (use unless requirements specify otherwise):\n**Web Apps**: React/Vue frontend + Python/Node backend + PostgreSQL\n**APIs**: REST with OpenAPI docs, JWT auth\n**Deployment**: Docker containers + cloud platform\n**Testing**: Jest/pytest, CI/CD with GitHub Actions\n**State**: Redux/Vuex for complex UIs, Context API for simple\n\n1. **Read Requirements**: Use read_file to thoroughly understand the requirements document\n\n2. **Analyze Constraints**: Consider:\nFunctional requirements (what must it do?)\nNon-functional requirements (performance, scale, security)\nTechnical constraints (languages, platforms, existing systems)\nResource constraints (time, complexity, cost)\n\n3. **Design Architecture**: Create a comprehensive proposal with these sections:\n\n   **a) Architecture Overview**\nHigh-level system design\nArchitecture pattern (e.g., MVC, microservices, layered)\nRationale for chosen approach\n\n   **b) Tech Stack**\nLanguages and frameworks\nLibraries and dependencies\nTools and platforms\n**For each choice, explain WHY and what alternatives were considered**\n\n   **c) System Components**\nComponent breakdown\nResponsibility of each component\nHow components interact\nData flow diagrams (in text/markdown)\n\n   **d) File/Directory Structure**\nProposed directory layout\nModule organization\nConfiguration file locations\n\n   **e) Data Model** (if applicable)\nDatabase schema\nData structures\nState management approach\n\n   **f) API Design** (if applicable)\nEndpoint structure\nRequest/response formats\nAuthentication approach\n\n   **g) Deployment Strategy**\nHow will this be deployed?\nEnvironment configuration\nCI/CD considerations\n\n   **h) Testing Strategy**\nUnit testing approach\nIntegration testing approach\nHow to verify requirements are met\n\n   **i) Alternatives Considered**\nWhat other approaches were considered?\nWhy were they rejected?\nTrade-offs of chosen approach\n\n   **j) Risks and Mitigations**\nPotential risks in this architecture\nHow to mitigate them\n\n   **k) Open Questions**\nDecisions that need user input\nTrade-offs where user preference matters\n\n4. **Write the Proposal**: Use write_file to create a clear, comprehensive markdown document\n\n5. **Retur